Answer generated:
"Paging enables virtual memory by allowing the system to use a smaller amount of physical memory than is actually available. This is achieved through the concept of "virtual addresses" which are mapped to "physical addresses." When a process needs more memory than it has allocated, it can request additional pages from the operating system, which then maps these pages into virtual memory.

The limitation of physical memory that paging addresses is that it allows processes to use more memory than they have physically available. This means that even if there isn't enough physical RAM for all the data a process needs, the OS can still map those pages into virtual memory and provide them as needed. This effectively extends the usable memory capacity of the system beyond what's actually present in physical RAM.

So, paging allows processes to use more memory than is physically available by mapping these additional pages into virtual memory, thereby addressing the limitation of physical memory size."

