Answer generated:
"Paging enables virtual memory by allowing the system to use a portion of the disk space as if it were part of the main memory. This is achieved through the concept of page tables, which map logical addresses (virtual addresses) to physical addresses in the main memory.

The limitation of physical memory that paging addresses is that it does not provide direct access to all the data stored on the hard drive. Instead, it allows for efficient use of disk space by breaking down large files into smaller pages and storing them in a contiguous block of memory. This means that while virtual memory can be accessed as if it were main memory, some parts of the file may still need to be read from or written to the disk.

In summary, paging enables virtual memory by allowing access to secondary storage as if it were primary memory through page tables and physical addressing, thereby addressing the limitation of physical memory's inability to directly address all data on a hard drive."

