Answer generated:
"Paging in virtual memory enables the mapping of a large amount of physical memory into smaller pages that can fit within the available physical memory. This technique allows for efficient use of limited physical memory by dividing it into manageable chunks called pages.

The limitation of physical memory is that it has a finite size, and as processes grow larger or more complex, they may exceed this limit. Paging addresses this issue by allowing virtual memory to be used even when the actual physical memory is insufficient. By mapping large portions of secondary storage (like disk) into virtual memory using pages, paging allows programs to access these resources without needing an equivalent amount of physical RAM.

This method effectively extends the available address space beyond what can be physically addressed in main memory, thus addressing one of the limitations of traditional memory management techniques that rely solely on physical memory."

