Answer generated:
"According to OS_Notes.txt, paging is a technique used in virtual memory management where data is stored temporarily on disk rather than in physical RAM. This allows the operating system to manage more memory resources by using a portion of the hard drive as an additional source of storage for applications and processes that exceed the available physical memory. Paging involves mapping pages (sections) of a process's address space into virtual addresses, which are then translated into physical addresses on disk when needed. This technique helps in managing limited main memory by allowing processes to access larger amounts of data than can be physically stored in RAM at any given time."

