Answer generated:
"Paging is a technique used in virtual memory management where pages are divided into smaller units called frames. When a program needs more space than what it has allocated, the operating system allocates additional frames from the disk. This allows programs to access larger amounts of data without increasing physical memory usage.

Page faults occur when a process tries to read or write a page that is not currently in RAM but is stored on disk. The operating system detects this and requests the corresponding page from the disk, which then replaces an older version of the same page into RAM. This ensures that all pages are kept up-to-date with their most recent versions.

In summary, paging allows programs to use more memory than what's physically available by allocating additional frames from disk when needed, while page faults ensure that these extra pages remain in RAM and can be accessed quickly."

