Answer generated:
"Paging enables virtual memory by allowing the system to use a smaller amount of physical memory than is actually available. This is achieved through the concept of page tables, which map logical addresses (virtual addresses) to physical addresses in the main memory.

The limitation of physical memory that paging addresses is that it does not provide direct access to all pages simultaneously. Instead, pages are accessed one at a time as they are needed by processes. This means that while virtual memory allows for more efficient use of physical memory compared to using only a fixed amount of RAM, it also requires the system to manage and switch between different sets of pages.

In summary, paging enables virtual memory by allowing logical addresses (virtual) to be mapped to physical addresses in main memory, thereby addressing the limitation of physical memory that is not sufficient for all processes."

