"Virtual Memory, also known as Virtual Storage or Virtual Address Space, is a memory management technique used by operating systems. It creates an illusion of having more main memory than actually exists on the system. This allows programs to access data that resides in secondary storage (such as hard drives) without needing to be physically present in RAM.

Virtual Memory works by mapping pages of physical memory into virtual addresses, which can then be accessed just like any other part of the main memory. When a program tries to read or write to a page that is not currently in RAM, it triggers a "page fault" exception. This causes the operating system to load the required data from disk into RAM and update the mapping table.

The correct answer to the question "What is virtual memory?" based on the provided context is:

A. A memory management technique that creates an illusion of a large main memory."

