"Paging is a memory management scheme that eliminates the need for contiguous allocation of physical memory by using separate pages of virtual memory. It works by mapping each process's address space into a set of fixed-size pages in main memory. This allows processes to access any part of their code or data without needing to know the exact location of the page in physical memory, which can be very large and fragmented.

The problem it solves is that traditional memory allocation methods often require contiguous blocks of memory, but this can lead to inefficiencies when dealing with larger systems where fragmentation becomes a significant issue. Paging allows processes to use any part of their address space without worrying about finding the exact location in physical memory, thus improving efficiency and reducing overhead.

Answer: Paging solves the problem of efficient memory management by allowing processes to access any part of their virtual address space using fixed-size pages instead of contiguous blocks. This eliminates the need for large, fragmented physical memory that can lead to inefficiencies due to fragmentation."

