Answer generated:
"Virtual memory management involves using the physical memory and secondary storage (such as hard disk) together to provide a larger address space than is available in physical memory. When an application requests more memory than is currently allocated, it is swapped out of physical memory onto the secondary storage. This allows the program to continue running even when there isn't enough physical memory for all its processes.

Virtual memory pages are used as units that can be moved between physical and virtual address spaces. The operating system manages these pages by swapping them in or out based on their usage, ensuring efficient use of both types of memory resources.

In summary, virtual memory allows the computer to access secondary storage just like it does with primary memory, making it appear as if all memory is available at any time. This technique optimizes resource utilization and improves system performance by leveraging the capabilities of both physical and secondary storage."

