"Virtual Memory is a memory management technique where the operating system creates an illusion of having more main memory than actually exists. This allows programs to access data even when that data is not currently stored in physical RAM.

Paging enables virtual memory by dividing the main memory into fixed-size blocks called pages, and mapping these pages onto different locations in the main memory as needed. When a program tries to access a page of memory that is not currently present in RAM, it triggers a "page fault" which causes the operating system to load the required data from disk into RAM.

A page fault occurs when a program attempts to access a block of memory that is not stored in RAM. This can happen for several reasons: the program may have requested more memory than is available, or the program may be accessing an area of memory that has been swapped out to disk. When this happens, the operating system intercepts the request and loads the required data from disk into RAM before continuing execution.

In summary, virtual memory allows programs to access data even when it is not currently in physical memory by creating a "virtual" main memory that can be expanded as needed. Paging enables this by dividing the main memory into pages and mapping them onto different locations as needed, while page faults occur when a program attempts to access a page of memory that is not present in RAM."

