"Virtual Memory (VM) is a memory management technique that creates an illusion of having more main memory than actually exists. It allows programs to access data even when the physical memory is full or when they need additional space.

Paging enables virtual memory by dividing the main memory into fixed-size blocks called pages, which can be accessed independently from each other. When a program tries to read or write a page that is not currently in RAM, it triggers a Page Fault exception. The operating system then retrieves the required data from disk and places it in RAM before continuing execution.

A Page Fault occurs when a program attempts to access a block of memory that is not stored in RAM. This typically happens because the program has requested more space than can be held in physical memory, or because the page containing the requested information is currently absent from RAM. The operating system intercepts this request and performs an I/O operation to retrieve the required data from disk before allowing the program to continue executing.

In summary:

- Virtual Memory: Creates an illusion of a large main memory.
- Paging: Divides main memory into fixed-size pages for independent access.
- Page Fault: Occurs when a program attempts to access a page that is not in RAM, triggering I/O operation."

