Answer generated:
"Paging is a technique used in virtual memory management where pages are allocated from disk space instead of physical RAM. When a program needs more data than can fit into the available memory, it requests additional pages from the operating system. A page fault occurs when the requested page is not currently present in main memory.

A page fault happens when a process attempts to access a virtual address that does not correspond to an actual location in physical memory. The kernel intercepts this request and retrieves the required data from disk or swap space, then updates the translation table so future accesses can be satisfied without further faults. This mechanism allows programs to use more memory than is physically available by using disk as a secondary storage solution.

Page faults are essential for managing virtual memory effectively, ensuring that processes have access to all necessary information even when they exceed their allocated physical memory limits."

