Answer generated:
"Paging is a technique used in virtual memory management where pages are divided into smaller units called frames. When a program needs more space than what it has allocated, the operating system allocates additional frames from the disk. This process of allocating new frames to a running program is known as page fault.

A page fault occurs when the address translation fails and the kernel cannot find the requested data in memory or on disk. The operating system then reads the required data into memory before continuing execution. Page faults are an essential part of virtual memory management, ensuring that programs can access all their needed resources even if they exceed the available physical memory.

In summary, paging is a method for managing large amounts of memory by dividing it into smaller pages and using disk space to store these pages temporarily when necessary. Page faults occur whenever the operating system needs to read data from disk because the requested page is not in memory."

