"Virtual Memory is a technique used by operating systems to allow programs to use more memory than is physically available. It creates an illusion of having a larger main memory by using disk space as if it were RAM.

Paging works by dividing the virtual address space into fixed-size segments called pages, and mapping each page to a specific location in physical memory or on disk. When a program tries to access a page that is not currently in memory, a page fault occurs, indicating that the page needs to be loaded from disk before it can be executed.

Page faults are needed because programs often need to access data that has been moved between different parts of memory due to fragmentation or other reasons. By catching these page faults and reloading the necessary pages into memory, virtual memory allows programs to continue running even when some of their required resources are temporarily unavailable."

