"Paging is a technique used in virtual memory management where pages are divided into smaller units called frames. When a program needs more space than what it has allocated, the operating system allocates additional frames from the disk. This allows programs to access larger amounts of data without increasing physical memory usage.

Page faults occur when a process tries to access a page that is not currently in memory but is present on the disk. The operating system detects this fault and retrieves the required page from the disk, then updates the virtual address space mapping so that future accesses can be satisfied by memory instead of disk. This helps maintain efficient use of physical memory while allowing programs to run without being limited by their available RAM size."

